2026-06-06 12:25:14,838 TITLE: Effect of hydrothermal pretreatment on two-stage anaerobic digestion of food waste and Enteromorpha: Digestion performance, bioenergy efficiency, and microbial community dynamics Title of processing article
2026-06-06 12:27:18,557 {
  "pretreatments": [
    {
      "id": 0,
      "description": "Hydrothermal Pretreatment (HTP) was conducted in a 1000 mL stainless-steel autoclave reactor (WZC-1000) with a Teflon lining. The total volatile solids (VS) concentration was set at 30 g VS·L^-1, with a FW-to-Enteromorpha ratio of 3:1 on a VS basis. Fresh Enteromorpha and FW were pulverized to approximately 0.25 mm and less than 0.21 mm respectively using a hammer mill (YSW-180, Zhengde, China). Impurities such as bones, plastic bags, and plastic cups were removed from the FW. Oyster shells, collected from Binhai New Area, Tianjin, China, were added to a total dry weight of 8% (w/w) of FW and Enteromorpha to strengthen the system, stabilize pH, and improve the performance of DF. The moisture content was adjusted to 90% using deionized water. The reactor was heated to 50, 80, 110, or 140 °C and maintained for 20 min. The HTP process was carried out to enhance digestion performance and obtain higher bioenergy efficiency, with chemical-free nature and environment-friendly nature noted."
    },
    {
      "id": 1,
      "description": "Oyster shells (collected from Binhai New Area, Tianjin, China) were pulverized to less than 0.21 mm using a hammer mill (YSW-180, Zhengde, China). They were added to the total dry weight of FW and Enteromorpha at 8% (w/w) of the total dry weight. This addition was performed to strengthen the system, stabilize pH, and improve the performance of DF, ensuring suitable buffering capacity for hydrogen generation during anaerobic digestion."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "description": "Mechanical degradation (grinding/milling) was performed on substrates during sample preparation. The FW was milled using a food grinder (Element40, BECBAS, China), with bones, plastic bags, and plastic cups removed. Fresh Enteromorpha was collected from the seaside of Laoshan District, Qingdao, Shandong, China, air-dried, then pulverized to approximately 0.25 mm using a hammer mill (YSW-180, Zhengde, China). Oyster shells were pulverized to less than 0.21 mm using a hammer mill (YSW-180, Zhengde, China). This mechanical degradation was necessary to ensure the substrates were suitable for anaerobic digestion."
    }
  ]
}
