2026-06-06 15:13:50,653 articletxt/1463.txt
2026-06-06 15:13:50,654 TITLE: Combined treatment of alkaline and disperser for improving solubilization and anaerobic biodegradability of dairy waste activated sludge Title of processing article
2026-06-06 15:15:56,907 {
  "pretreatments": [
    {
      "id": 0,
      "description": "Alkaline pretreatment was applied to dairy waste activated sludge using NaOH, KOH, and Ca(OH)2 of different concentrations at pH values ranging from 8 to 11. The chemical treatment was performed as batch tests with a volume of 500 mL sludge sample at room temperature, without temperature regulation, to weaken the cell walls and make cellular substances more susceptible to enzymatic action. The alkaline agents induced swelling of particulate organics at high pH, enhancing hydrolysis of cell structures and microbial cell wall degradation. The effect was evaluated at different treatment times (15-180 min) and various working rpm ranges (4000–24,000 rpm) to optimize sludge disintegration and solubilization. The specific alkaline agents (NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)2) were selected for subsequent studies as NaOH was found to be more effective than Ca(OH)2 for sludge solubilization due to the bivalent cation's role in dissolving organic polymers, and Ca(OH)2 was noted to have reduced COD solubilization efficiency compared to NaOH."
    },
    {
      "id": 1,
      "description": "Mechanical pretreatment (disperser) was applied using the IKA T25 Ultra Turrax Digital disperser. This method relies on the induced cavitation process where high-speed shearing forces disintegrate the sludge by disrupting microbial cell walls through shear stress and extensional shear. The dispersion effectiveness was heavily dependent on the product of shear gradient and time the particles spent in the shear zone. The disperser draws the medium axially and forces it radially through the slots in the rotor/stator arrangement, creating extreme shear and thrust forces that break down flocs and release soluble substances into the liquid phase. This treatment was used in conjunction with alkaline pretreatment to achieve combined chemo-mechanical disintegration, reducing viscosity and increasing solids concentration for anaerobic digestion."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "description": "Combined chemo-mechanical pretreatment was applied to dairy waste activated sludge by combining alkaline chemical treatment with disperser mechanical treatment. The optimal specific energy input of 4544 kJ/kg TS was selected for NaOH pretreatment at pH 10 to maximize sludge disintegration efficiency. The combined method was trialed at the Chilliwack WWTP and resulted in improved VS reduction in mesophilic digesters by 18% points from 60% to 78%. The combined pretreatment improved COD solubilization and suspended solids reduction, enhanced hydrolysis, and led to higher biogas production. The optimized parameters for this combined treatment included specific energy input of 4544 kJ/kg TS, NaOH at pH 10, and treatment time of 15-180 minutes."
    }
  ]
}
2026-06-06 15:19:46,570 articletxt/1619.txt
2026-06-06 15:19:46,570 TITLE: TiO2 - chitosan thin film induced solar photocatalytic deflocculation of sludge for profitable bacterial pretreatment and biofuel production Title of processing article
2026-06-06 15:21:15,774 {
  "pretreatments": [
    {
      "id": 0,
      "description": "Solar photocatalytic deflocculation using TiO2/chitosan thin film (TCTF) embedded in the sludge substrate. This treatment involves impregnating TiO2 nanoparticles into a chitosan nanocomposite matrix, followed by solar irradiation to facilitate exopolymer (EPS) removal. The process utilizes an optimized dosage of TiO2 (0.05 g/g SS) and chitosan to disrupt microbial aggregates, reduce turbidity, and expose microbial cells for subsequent enzymatic action. The deflocculation efficiency was optimized to ensure complete disruption of EPS layers, resulting in a 99.35% deflocculation degree within 25 minutes, which allows for effective exposure of microbial cells for bacterial disintegration."
    },
    {
      "id": 1,
      "description": "Enzyme secreting bacterial disintegration of deflocculated sludge. This pretreatment utilizes specific bacterial strains isolated and identified as Bacillus jerish 03 (Accession number: KC597266) and Bacillus jerish 04 (Accession number: KC597267). The treated sludge is incubated at 40 °C under constant mixing for 42 hours at a pH of 6.5. This process releases protease and amylase enzymes from the bacterial cells to solubilize particulate organic matter. The treatment significantly enhances SS reduction from 3914.58 mg/L to 3500 mg/L, increases COD solubilization efficiency to 16%, and promotes maximum intracellular substance release (Protein: 452.48 mg/L, Carbohydrate: 290.88 mg/L) before anaerobic digestion."
    }
  ]
}
